From 21ec32a66678e5eb4fdb3d619b6eadbabc85d69b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 5 Jul 2026 17:15:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH net-next v1 15/15] net: flow_dissector: add KUnit fast/slow
 path equivalence tests

The per-shape fast paths contract to be byte-identical with the slow
path: for any packet, either struct flow_keys is filled with exactly
the bytes the slow path would have written, or the fast path returns
false and the slow path runs. Until now that contract was only
verified out of tree (a userspace A/B harness compiling the dissector
both ways). Enforce it in-tree so any future divergence between the
two implementations is a failing test, not a silent behaviour change.

Each corpus packet is dissected twice into zeroed containers - once
with every fast-path gate disabled and once with every byte-identical
gate enabled - and the return values and full struct flow_keys must
match exactly (the zeroed containers also prove a fast-path miss never
leaves partial writes behind). The corpus covers the eligible shapes
(eth+IPv4/IPv6 x TCP/UDP, VLAN, QinQ, PPPoE session, single MPLS,
IPIP/4in6/6in4, plain GRE), deliberate fast-path misses (IP options,
fragments with and without FLOW_DISSECTOR_F_PARSE_1ST_FRAG, IPv6
extension headers and non-zero flow labels, triple VLAN, multi-label
MPLS, non-IP PPP, flagged GRE, ICMP), a truncation sweep cutting every
packet at every byte boundary, and skb-mode cases for the
hardware-stripped VLAN tag entry conditions raw-data mode cannot
reach.

The UDP-tunnel descents (vxlan/geneve/gtpu/fou/gue inner) are covered
too. Because a descent changes the output relative to not descending,
the equivalence is checked the other way round: the descent gate is
pinned on and the entry gates are toggled, so one pass descends via the
fast path and the other descends via the slow path (the fast
dispatcher defers with the entry gates off). Both must produce the same
flow_keys. The corpus exercises inner TCP/UDP, IPv6-outer/IPv4-inner
residue for fou/gue, inner ICMP and Geneve/GTP-U out-of-subset packets
(both keep the outer keys), and a nested VXLAN chain past
MAX_FLOW_DISSECT_HDRS (both bail at the same depth). A dummy netdev and
a mock flow_dissector_fou_ops provider drive the FOU/GUE lookup without
real fou config.

Run with:

  ./tools/testing/kunit/kunit.py run --arch=x86_64 \
      --kconfig_add CONFIG_NET=y \
      --kconfig_add CONFIG_FLOW_DISSECTOR_KUNIT_TEST=y \
      flow_dissector_fastpath

Signed-off-by: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
---
 net/Kconfig                    |   12 +
 net/core/Makefile              |    1 +
 net/core/flow_dissector.c      |   13 +
 net/core/flow_dissector_test.c | 1448 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 1474 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 net/core/flow_dissector_test.c

diff --git a/net/Kconfig b/net/Kconfig
index e38477393551..d0ce8fc34429 100644
--- a/net/Kconfig
+++ b/net/Kconfig
@@ -540,4 +540,16 @@ config NET_TEST
 
 	  If unsure, say N.
 
